          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is described as first-line drug therapy for type 2 diabetes?",
          "answer": "For individuals with type 2 diabetes who do not have other compelling health conditions, metformin is generally recommended as the initial drug therapy, along with structured lifestyle changes. This recommendation is based on its long history of use, low cost, neutral effect on weight, and low risk of causing low blood sugar on its own.\n\nCurrent guidance emphasizes that the first-line treatment choice can depend on other conditions a person might have. Kidney function is checked before starting metformin and monitored periodically afterward, as the dose may need adjustment or the medication avoided depending on kidney function.\n\nThis information comes from the \"National diabetes association standards of care\" as summarized in the document \"Type 2 diabetes: first-line pharmacologic management.\"\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
